99" :question/math-academy-id "q-148330"] ["question-1e6dcf9d-d6cd-51ac-b73d-58cf9701a199" :question/problem "For two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=0.24,$ $\\text{Var}[Y]=0.06,$ and $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=0.08.$ Find $\\text{Var}[2X-8Y].$"] ["question-1e6dcf9d-d6cd-51ac-b73d-58cf9701a199" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y]+2ab\\,\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[2X-8Y] & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 0.24+(-8)^{2}\\cdot 0.06+2\\cdot 2\\cdot (-8)\\cdot 0.08 \\\\  & =0.96+3.84-2.56 \\\\  & =2.24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["question-1e6dcf9d-d6cd-51ac-b73d-58cf9701a199" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-b2574087-a149-5760-a3e0-95a498fe4957" :answer-field/id #uuid "b2574087-a149-5760-a3e0-95a498fe4957"] ["answer-909907e4-2fff-5429-908d-172891d594e7" :answer/id #uuid "909907e4-2fff-5429-908d-172891d594e7"] ["answer-909907e4-2fff-5429-908d-172891d594e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-909907e4-2fff-5429-908d-172891d594e7" :answer/value "1.18"] ["answer-3cdc858f-23d2-52f7-803a-0d8ca2c80206" :answer/id #uuid "3cdc858f-23d2-52f7-803a-0d8ca2c80206"] ["answer-3cdc858f-23d2-52f7-803a-0d8ca2c80206" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-3cdc858f-23d2-52f7-803a-0d8ca2c80206" :answer/value "1.51"] ["answer-60036dd0-65af-52cb-a069-bd37caf7adfd" :answer/id #uuid "60036dd0-65af-52cb-a069-bd37caf7adfd"] ["answer-60036dd0-65af-52cb-a069-bd37caf7adfd" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-60036dd0-65af-52cb-a069-bd37caf7adfd" :answer/value "1.78"] ["answer-a7330edf-fd61-584c-a0ef-021af49ab7e0" :answer/id #uuid "a7330edf-fd61-584c-a0ef-021af49ab7e0"] ["answer-a7330edf-fd61-584c-a0ef-021af49ab7e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-a7330edf-fd61-584c-a0ef-021af49ab7e0" :answer/value "2.24"] ["answer-1730b466-edec-5557-8be0-4765900d97d7" :answer/id #uuid "1730b466-edec-5557-8be0-4765900d97d7"] ["answer-1730b466-edec-5557-8be0-4765900d97d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-1730b466-edec-5557-8be0-4765900d97d7" :answer/value "2.53"] ["answer-field-b2574087-a149-5760-a3e0-95a498fe4957" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-b2574087-a149-5760-a3e0-95a498fe4957" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-b2574087-a149-5760-a3e0-95a498fe4957" :answer-field/correct "answer-a7330edf-fd61-584c-a0ef-021af49ab7e0"] ["answer-field-b2574087-a149-5760-a3e0-95a498fe4957" :answer-field/choices "answer-909907e4-2fff-5429-908d-172891d594e7"] ["answer-field-b2574087-a149-5760-a3e0-95a498fe4957" :answer-field/choices "answer-3cdc858f-23d2-52f7-803a-0d8ca2c80206"] ["answer-field-b2574087-a149-5760-a3e0-95a498fe4957" :answer-field/choices "answer-60036dd0-65af-52cb-a069-bd37caf7adfd"] ["answer-field-b2574087-a149-5760-a3e0-95a498fe4957" :answer-field/choices "answer-a7330edf-fd61-584c-a0ef-021af49ab7e0"] ["answer-field-b2574087-a149-5760-a3e0-95a498fe4957" :answer-field/choices "answer-1730b466-edec-5557-8be0-4765900d97d7"] ["question-1e6dcf9d-d6cd-51ac-b73d-58cf9701a199" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-b2574087-a149-5760-a3e0-95a498fe4957"] [17592186188359 :knowledge-point/questions "question-1e6dcf9d-d6cd-51ac-b73d-58cf9701a199"] [17592186188787 :question/problem "The joint probability mass function of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.5$ |\n\nFind $\\text{Var}[2X+3Y].$"] [17592186188787 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y]+2ab\\,\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nAlso, the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.3$ | $0.5$ | $0.8$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.2+1\\cdot 0.8 \\\\  & =0.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.2+{1}^{2}\\cdot 0.8 \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =0.8-{0.8}^{2} \\\\  & =0.16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.4+2\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =1.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.4+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =2.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =2.8-{1.6}^{2} \\\\  & =0.24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ We compute $\\text{E}[XY]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1\\cdot f(0,1)+0\\cdot 2\\cdot f(0,2)+1\\cdot 1\\cdot f(1,1)+1\\cdot 2\\cdot f(1,2) \\\\  & =0+0+1\\cdot 0.3+2\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =1.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[XY] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =1.3-0.8\\cdot 1.6 \\\\  & =0.02.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[2X+3Y] & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 0.16+{3}^{2}\\cdot 0.24+2\\cdot 2\\cdot 3\\cdot 0.02 \\\\  & =0.64+2.16+0.24 \\\\  & =3.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186188787 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard] [17592186187002 :answer-field/correct 17592186187004] ["question-43034259-a22b-5dd7-b48f-a2ed42e0b13d" :question/id #uuid "43034259-a22b-5dd7-b48f-a2ed42e0b13d"] ["question-43034259-a22b-5dd7-b48f-a2ed42e0b13d" :question/math-academy-id "q-148346"] ["question-43034259-a22b-5dd7-b48f-a2ed42e0b13d" :question/problem "The joint probability mass function of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.4$ | $0.3$ |\n\nFind $\\text{Var}[3X-Y].$"] ["question-43034259-a22b-5dd7-b48f-a2ed42e0b13d" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y]+2ab\\,\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nAlso, the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.4$ | $0.3$ | $0.7$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.5$ | $0.5$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.3+2\\cdot 0.7 \\\\  & =1.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.3+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.7 \\\\  & =2.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =2.8-{1.4}^{2} \\\\  & =0.84.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.5+1\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{1}^{2}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =0.5-{0.5}^{2} \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ We find $\\text{E}[XY]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0\\cdot f(0,0)+0\\cdot 1\\cdot f(0,1)+2\\cdot 0\\cdot f(2,0)+2\\cdot 1\\cdot f(2,1) \\\\  & =0+0+0+2\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[XY] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =0.6-1.4\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =-0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[3X-Y] & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 0.84+(-1)^{2}\\cdot 0.25+2\\cdot 3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-0.1) \\\\  & =7.56+0.25+0.6 \\\\  & =8.41.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["question-43034259-a22b-5dd7-b48f-a2ed42e0b13d" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard] ["answer-field-338abd00-bef7-5a0e-90d2-b18f076c9de2" :answer-field/id #uuid "338abd00-bef7-5a0e-90d2-b18f076c9de2"] ["answer-49ada7d0-4cef-56d8-bcef-b0502f40aca2" :answer/id #uuid "49ada7d0-4cef-56d8-bcef-b0502f40aca2"] ["answer-49ada7d0-4cef-56d8-bcef-b0502f40aca2" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-49ada7d0-4cef-56d8-bcef-b0502f40aca2" :answer/value "5.69"] ["answer-468fa4dd-aa55-5c1c-adbf-af8f95c32adb" :answer/id #uuid "468fa4dd-aa55-5c1c-adbf-af8f95c32adb"] ["answer-468fa4dd-aa55-5c1c-adbf-af8f95c32adb" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-468fa4dd-aa55-5c1c-adbf-af8f95c32adb" :answer/value "6.32"] ["answer-720fce8b-4a4c-5001-875c-151d8e621507" :answer/id #uuid "720fce8b-4a4c-5001-875c-151d8e621507"] ["answer-720fce8b-4a4c-5001-875c-151d8e621507" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-720fce8b-4a4c-5001-875c-151d8e621507" :answer/value "8.11"] ["answer-8b1cefcd-2445-53e0-9e6c-8133a9228e46" :answer/id #uuid "8b1cefcd-2445-53e0-9e6c-8133a9228e46"] ["answer-8b1cefcd-2445-53e0-9e6c-8133a9228e46" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-8b1cefcd-2445-53e0-9e6c-8133a9228e46" :answer/value "8.41"] ["answer-5470918e-e06a-57bb-b01a-7b0ef04bc1bb" :answer/id #uuid "5470918e-e06a-57bb-b01a-7b0ef04bc1bb"] ["answer-5470918e-e06a-57bb-b01a-7b0ef04bc1bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-5470918e-e06a-57bb-b01a-7b0ef04bc1bb" :answer/value "9.26"] ["answer-field-338abd00-bef7-5a0e-90d2-b18f076c9de2" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-338abd00-bef7-5a0e-90d2-b18f076c9de2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-338abd00-bef7-5a0e-90d2-b18f076c9de2" :answer-field/correct "answer-8b1cefcd-2445-53e0-9e6c-8133a9228e46"] ["answer-field-338abd00-bef7-5a0e-90d2-b18f076c9de2" :answer-field/choices "answer-49ada7d0-4cef-56d8-bcef-b0502f40aca2"] ["answer-field-338abd00-bef7-5a0e-90d2-b18f076c9de2" :answer-field/choices "answer-468fa4dd-aa55-5c1c-adbf-af8f95c32adb"] ["answer-field-338abd00-bef7-5a0e-90d2-b18f076c9de2" :answer-field/choices "answer-720fce8b-4a4c-5001-875c-151d8e621507"] ["answer-field-338abd00-bef7-5a0e-90d2-b18f076c9de2" :answer-field/choices "answer-8b1cefcd-2445-53e0-9e6c-8133a9228e46"] ["answer-field-338abd00-bef7-5a0e-90d2-b18f076c9de2" :answer-field/choices "answer-5470918e-e06a-57bb-b01a-7b0ef04bc1bb"] ["question-43034259-a22b-5dd7-b48f-a2ed42e0b13d" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-338abd00-bef7-5a0e-90d2-b18f076c9de2"] [17592186188360 :knowledge-point/questions "question-43034259-a22b-5dd7-b48f-a2ed42e0b13d"] ["question-7c291da1-e168-5e62-8128-2d4c3d94af53" :question/id #uuid "7c291da1-e168-5e62-8128-2d4c3d94af53"] ["question-7c291da1-e168-5e62-8128-2d4c3d94af53" :question/math-academy-id "q-148351"] ["question-7c291da1-e168-5e62-8128-2d4c3d94af53" :question/problem "The joint probability mass function of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.3$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.35$ |\n\nFind $\\text{Var}[3X+4Y].$"] ["question-7c291da1-e168-5e62-8128-2d4c3d94af53" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y]+2ab\\,\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nAlso, the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.3$ | $0.15$ | $0.45$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.35$ | $0.55$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.5$ | $0.5$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.45+4\\cdot 0.55 \\\\  & =2.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.45+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.55 \\\\  & =8.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =8.8-{2.2}^{2} \\\\  & =3.96.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.5+2\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =2.5-{1.5}^{2} \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ We find $\\text{E}[XY]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1\\cdot f(0,1)+0\\cdot 2\\cdot f(0,2)+4\\cdot 1\\cdot f(4,1)+4\\cdot 2\\cdot f(4,2) \\\\  & =0+0+4\\cdot 0.2+8\\cdot 0.35 \\\\  & =3.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[XY] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =3.6-2.2\\cdot 1.5 \\\\  & =0.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[3X+4Y] & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 3.96+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.25+2\\cdot 3\\cdot 4\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =35.64+4+7.2 \\\\  & =46.84.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["question-7c291da1-e168-5e62-8128-2d4c3d94af53" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard] ["answer-field-a4a349e6-751b-52e3-889b-23f3be2178b3" :answer-field/id #uuid "a4a349e6-751b-52e3-889b-23f3be2178b3"] ["answer-b594c8db-9430-5a22-b797-08e58983a322" :answer/id #uuid "b594c8db-9430-5a22-b797-08e58983a322"] ["answer-b594c8db-9430-5a22-b797-08e58983a322" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-b594c8db-9430-5a22-b797-08e58983a322" :answer/value "32.56"] ["answer-c7b3b1e0-de02-59a0-8905-c06056522de3" :answer/id #uuid "c7b3b1e0-de02-59a0-8905-c06056522de3"] ["answer-c7b3b1e0-de02-59a0-8905-c06056522de3" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-c7b3b1e0-de02-59a0-8905-c06056522de3" :answer/value "39.62"] ["answer-bb3807e9-784b-5046-9fb0-1d5c63a1fe7f" :answer/id #uuid "bb3807e9-784b-5046-9fb0-1d5c63a1fe7f"] ["answer-bb3807e9-784b-5046-9fb0-1d5c63a1fe7f" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-bb3807e9-784b-5046-9fb0-1d5c63a1fe7f" :answer/value "43.86"] ["answer-eb7482b1-371a-5ba6-941f-1144ea4bfa78" :answer/id #uuid "eb7482b1-371a-5ba6-941f-1144ea4bfa78"] ["answer-eb7482b1-371a-5ba6-941f-1144ea4bfa78" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-eb7482b1-371a-5ba6-941f-1144ea4bfa78" :answer/value "46.84"] ["answer-2e3129f4-5fbf-5a1b-8819-be0cbbf448d2" :answer/id #uuid "2e3129f4-5fbf-5a1b-8819-be0cbbf448d2"] ["answer-2e3129f4-5fbf-5a1b-8819-be0cbbf448d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-2e3129f4-5fbf-5a1b-8819-be0cbbf448d2" :answer/value "54.12"] ["answer-field-a4a349e6-751b-52e3-889b-23f3be2178b3" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-a4a349e6-751b-52e3-889b-23f3be2178b3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-a4a349e6-751b-52e3-889b-23f3be2178b3" :answer-field/correct "answer-eb7482b1-371a-5ba6-941f-1144ea4bfa78"] ["answer-field-a4a349e6-751b-52e3-889b-23f3be2178b3" :answer-field/choices "answer-b594c8db-9430-5a22-b797-08e58983a322"] ["answer-field-a4a349e6-751b-52e3-889b-23f3be2178b3" :answer-field/choices "answer-c7b3b1e0-de02-59a0-8905-c06056522de3"] ["answer-field-a4a349e6-751b-52e3-889b-23f3be2178b3" :answer-field/choices "answer-bb3807e9-784b-5046-9fb0-1d5c63a1fe7f"] ["answer-field-a4a349e6-751b-52e3-889b-23f3be2178b3" :answer-field/choices "answer-eb7482b1-371a-5ba6-941f-1144ea4bfa78"] ["answer-field-a4a349e6-751b-52e3-889b-23f3be2178b3" :answer-field/choices "answer-2e3129f4-5fbf-5a1b-8819-be0cbbf448d2"] ["question-7c291da1-e168-5e62-8128-2d4c3d94af53" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-a4a349e6-751b-52e3-889b-23f3be2178b3"] [17592186188360 :knowledge-point/questions "question-7c291da1-e168-5e62-8128-2d4c3d94af53"] [17592186188786 :question/problem "Consider two random variables $X$ and $Y$ with expectations $\\text{E}[X]=2,$ $\\text{E}[Y]=4,$ mixed moment $\\text{E}[XY]=10,$ and second raw moments $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=9$ and $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]=20.$ Find the variance of $2X-5Y.$"] [17592186188786 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y]+2ab\\,\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nAlso, the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find $\\text{Var}[X]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =9-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =20-{4}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =10-2\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[2X-5Y] & ={2}^{2}\\cdot 5+(-5)^{2}\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 2\\cdot (-5)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =20+100-40 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186188786 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] [17592186186996 :answer-field/correct 17592186186997] ["question-e96228aa-7e46-5ce5-96d2-d22e87559f00" :question/id #uuid "e96228aa-7e46-5ce5-96d2-d22e87559f00"] ["question-e96228aa-7e46-5ce5-96d2-d22e87559f00" :question/math-academy-id "q-148348"] ["question-e96228aa-7e46-5ce5-96d2-d22e87559f00" :question/problem "The joint probability mass function of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.3$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0.25$ |\n\nFind $\\text{Var}[3X-2Y].$"] ["question-e96228aa-7e46-5ce5-96d2-d22e87559f00" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y]+2ab\\,\\text{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nAlso, the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2},\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.3$ | $0.25$ | $0.55$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0.25$ | $0.45$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.5$ | $0.5$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{X},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.55+2\\cdot 0.45 \\\\  & =0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & ={0}^{2}\\cdot 0.55+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.45 \\\\  & =1.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =1.8-{0.9}^{2} \\\\  & =0.99.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{Y},$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.5+2\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =1.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =2.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =2.5-{1.5}^{2} \\\\  & =0.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find $\\text{Cov}[X,Y].$ We find $\\text{E}[XY]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 1\\cdot f(0,1)+0\\cdot 2\\cdot f(0,2)+2\\cdot 1\\cdot f(2,1)+2\\cdot 2\\cdot f(2,2) \\\\  & =0+0+2\\cdot 0.2+4\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =1.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[XY] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =1.4-0.9\\cdot 1.5 \\\\  & =0.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[3X-2Y] & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 0.99+(-2)^{2}\\cdot 0.25+2\\cdot 3\\cdot (-2)\\cdot 0.05 \\\\  & =8.91+1-0.6 \\\\  & =9.31.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["question-e96228aa-7e46-5ce5-96d2-d22e87559f00" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard] ["answer-field-e551be10-49d7-5b00-a0da-a0e8ce37b373" :answer-field/id #uuid "e551be10-49d7-5b00-a0da-a0e8ce37b373"] ["answer-7f42f492-9fe9-5724-b584-66ae829fc7d9" :answer/id #uuid "7f42f492-9fe9-5724-b584-66ae829fc7d9"] ["answer-7f42f492-9fe9-5724-b584-66ae829fc7d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-7f42f492-9fe9-5724-b584-66ae829fc7d9" :answer/value "10.12"] ["answer-e7dcec73-2add-5548-aec0-fc739525b3dc" :answer/id #uuid "e7dcec73-2add-5548-aec0-fc739525b3dc"] ["answer-e7dcec73-2add-5548-aec0-fc739525b3dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-e7dcec73-2add-5548-aec0-fc739525b3dc" :answer/value "10.51"] ["answer-3f9906a6-c4a0-571b-a9fe-b0988e41fc63" :answer/id #uuid "3f9906a6-c4a0-571b-a9fe-b0988e41fc63"] ["answer-3f9906a6-c4a0-571b-a9fe-b0988e41fc63" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-3f9906a6-c4a0-571b-a9fe-b0988e41fc63" :answer/value "9.31"] ["answer-070e6987-47d6-5f73-9aa9-86a2d5cb82ec" :answer/id #uuid "070e6987-47d6-5f73-9aa9-86a2d5cb82ec"] ["answer-070e6987-47d6-5f73-9aa9-86a2d5cb82ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-070e6987-47d6-5f73-9aa9-86a2d5cb82ec" :answer/value "9.63"] ["answer-a7d64e3b-21c0-551e-b123-974f1cbd894d" :answer/id #uuid "a7d64e3b-21c0-551e-b123-974f1cbd894d"] ["answer-a7d64e3b-21c0-551e-b123-974f1cbd894d" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-a7d64e3b-21c0-551e-b123-974f1cbd894d" :answer/value "9.85"] ["answer-field-e551be10-49d7-5b00-a0da-a0e8ce37b373" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-e551be10-49d7-5b00-a0da-a0e8ce37b373" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-e551be10-49d7-5b00-a0da-a0e8ce37b373" :answer-field/correct "answer-3f9906a6-c4a0-571b-a9fe-b0988e41fc63"] ["answer-field-e551be10-49d7-5b00-a0da-a0e8ce37b373" :answer-field/choices "answer-7f42f492-9fe9-5724-b584-66ae829fc7d9"] ["answer-field-e551be10-49d7-5b00-a0da-a0e8ce37b373" :answer-field/choices "answer-e7dcec73-2add-5548-aec0-fc739525b3dc"] ["answer-field-e551be10-49d7-5b00-a0da-a0e8ce37b373" :answer-field/choices "answer-3f9906a6-c4a0-571b-a9fe-b0988e41fc63"] ["answer-field-e551be10-49d7-5b00-a0da-a0e8ce37b373" :answer-field/choices "answer-070e6987-47d6-5f73-9aa9-86a2d5cb82ec"] ["answer-field-e551be10-49d7-5b00-a0da-a0e8ce37b373" :answer-field/choices "answer-a7d64e3b-21c0-551e-b123-974f1cbd894d"] ["question-e96228aa-7e46-5ce5-96d2-d22e87559f00" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-e551be10-49d7-5b00-a0da-a0e8ce37b373"] [17592186188360 :knowledge-point/questions "question-e96228aa-7e46-5ce5-96d2-d22e87559f00"] ["question-e0b47f27-b72b-520b-9e5b-9f7678a818fc" :question/id #uuid "e0b47f27-b72b-520b-9e5b-9f7678a818fc"] ["question-e0b47f27-b72b-520b-9e5b-9f7678a818fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-148399"] ["question-e0b47f27-b72b-520b-9e5b-9f7678a818fc" :question/problem "For three random variables ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3},$ we have the following variances and covariances:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}]=3,\\,\\text{Var}[{X}_{2}]=0.5,\\,\\text{Var}[{X}_{3}]=1.5\\,\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]=-0.75,\\,\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{3}]=-3.5,\\,\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]=4\\,\n$$\n\nFind $\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}+4{X}_{2}+6{X}_{3}].$"] ["question-e0b47f27-b72b-520b-9e5b-9f7678a818fc" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},…,{X}_{n}$ are random variables, and ${a}_{1},…,{a}_{n}$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{a}_{i}{X}_{i}]={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{a}_{i}^{2}\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}]+2{\\sum }_{i<j}{a}_{i}{a}_{j}\\text{Cov}[{X}_{i},{X}_{j}].\n$$\n\nHence, for $n=3$ random variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[{a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3}] & ={a}_{1}^{2}\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}]+{a}_{2}^{2}\\text{Var}[{X}_{2}]+{a}_{3}^{2}\\text{Var}[{X}_{3}] \\\\  & +2{a}_{1}{a}_{2}\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]+2{a}_{1}{a}_{3}\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{3}]+2{a}_{2}{a}_{3}\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{3}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}+4{X}_{2}+6{X}_{3}] & ={1}^{2}\\cdot 3+{4}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{6}^{2}\\cdot 1.5 \\\\  & +2\\cdot 1\\cdot 4\\cdot (-0.75)+2\\cdot 1\\cdot 6\\cdot (-3.5)+2\\cdot 4\\cdot 6\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =3+8+54-6-42+192 \\\\  & =209.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] ["question-e0b47f27-b72b-520b-9e5b-9f7678a818fc" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-7013aeae-6a4b-54c0-a7ce-c4c928ca841d" :answer-field/id #uuid "7013aeae-6a4b-54c0-a7ce-c4c928ca841d"] ["answer-83f959c6-ecaf-52c2-83d2-c523d4096a30" :answer/id #uuid "83f959c6-ecaf-52c2-83d2-c523d4096a30"] ["answer-83f959c6-ecaf-52c2-83d2-c523d4096a30" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-83f959c6-ecaf-52c2-83d2-c523d4096a30" :answer/value "185"] ["answer-f22af125-b5c3-5190-b0ab-21b1cca3cf1e" :answer/id #uuid "f22af125-b5c3-5190-b0ab-21b1cca3cf1e"] ["answer-f22af125-b5c3-5190-b0ab-21b1cca3cf1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-f22af125-b5c3-5190-b0ab-21b1cca3cf1e" :answer/value "196"] ["answer-78534fc9-e405-57b5-9b6d-30c8b94d0b38" :answer/id #uuid "78534fc9-e405-57b5-9b6d-30c8b94d0b38"] ["answer-78534fc9-e405-57b5-9b6d-30c8b94d0b38" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-78534fc9-e405-57b5-9b6d-30c8b94d0b38" :answer/value "209"] ["answer-a508ac9a-d3b2-5d7e-9955-803b8f2e995c" :answer/id #uuid "a508ac9a-d3b2-5d7e-9955-803b8f2e995c"] ["answer-a508ac9a-d3b2-5d7e-9955-803b8f2e995c" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-a508ac9a-d3b2-5d7e-9955-803b8f2e995c" :answer/value "217"] ["answer-845e1070-adec-50b4-baf7-02a5443b23d9" :answer/id #uuid "845e1070-adec-50b4-baf7-02a5443b23d9"] ["answer-845e1070-adec-50b4-baf7-02a5443b23d9" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-845e1070-adec-50b4-baf7-02a5443b23d9" :answer/value "224"] ["answer-field-7013aeae-6a4b-54c0-a7ce-c4c928ca841d" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-7013aeae-6a4b-54c0-a7ce-c4c928ca841d" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-7013aeae-6a4b-54c0-a7ce-c4c928ca841d" :answer-field/correct "answer-78534fc9-e405-57b5-9b6d-30c8b94d0b38"] ["answer-field-7013aeae-6a4b-54c0-a7ce-c4c928ca841d" :answer-field/choices "answer-83f959c6-ecaf-52c2-83d2-c523d4096a30"] ["answer-field-7013aeae-6a4b-54c0-a7ce-c4c928ca841d" :answer-field/choices "answer-f22af125-b5c3-5190-b0ab-21b1cca3cf1e"] ["answer-field-7013aeae-6a4b-54c0-a7ce-c4c928ca841d" :answer-field/choices "answer-78534fc9-e405-57b5-9b6d-30c8b94d0b38"] ["answer-field-7013aeae-6a4b-54c0-a7ce-c4c928ca841d" :answer-field/choices "answer-a508ac9a-d3b2-5d7e-9955-803b8f2e995c"] ["answer-field-7013aeae-6a4b-54c0-a7ce-c4c928ca841d" :answer-field/choices "answer-845e1070-adec-50b4-baf7-02a5443b23d9"] ["question-e0b47f27-b72b-520b-9e5b-9f7678a818fc" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-7013aeae-6a4b-54c0-a7ce-c4c928ca841d"] [17592186188361 :knowledge-point/questions "question-e0b47f27-b72b-520b-9e5b-9f7678a818fc"] [17592186188784 :question/problem "For three random variables ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},$ and ${X}_{3},$ we have the following variances and covariances:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}]=5,\\,\\text{Var}[{X}_{2}]=4,\\,\\text{Var}[{X}_{3}]=2\\,\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]=3,\\,\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{3}]=1,\\,\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{3}]=2\\,\n$$\n\nFind $\\text{Var}[3{X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}+4{X}_{3}].$"] [17592186188784 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},…,{X}_{n}$ are random variables, and ${a}_{1},…,{a}_{n}$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{a}_{i}{X}_{i}]={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{a}_{i}^{2}\\text{Var}[{X}_{i}]+2{\\sum }_{i<j}{a}_{i}{a}_{j}\\text{Cov}[{X}_{i},{X}_{j}].\n$$\n\nHence, for $n=3$ random variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[{a}_{1}{X}_{1}+{a}_{2}{X}_{2}+{a}_{3}{X}_{3}] & ={a}_{1}^{2}\\text{Var}[{X}_{1}]+{a}_{2}^{2}\\text{Var}[{X}_{2}]+{a}_{3}^{2}\\text{Var}[{X}_{3}] \\\\  & +2{a}_{1}{a}_{2}\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{2}]+2{a}_{1}{a}_{3}\\text{Cov}[{X}_{1},{X}_{3}]+2{a}_{2}{a}_{3}\\text{Cov}[{X}_{2},{X}_{3}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[3{X}_{1}+2{X}_{2}+4{X}_{3}] & ={3}^{2}\\cdot 5+{2}^{2}\\cdot 4+{4}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & +2\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 3\\cdot 4\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2\\cdot 4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =45+16+32+36+24+32 \\\\  & =185.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186188784 :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] [17592186186984 :answer-field/correct 17592186186987] [17592186189413 :tutorial/content "Given two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ the variance of the sum or difference of these variables is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X\\pm Y] = \\textrm{Var}[X] + \\textrm{Var}[Y] \\pm 2\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y],\n$$\n\nwhere $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$ We'll prove this formula at the end of the lesson.\n\nNote that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]= 0,$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X\\pm Y] = \\textrm{Var}[X] + \\textrm{Var}[Y].\n$$"] [17592186189414 :tutorial/content "If $X$ and $Y$ are random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX + bY] = a^2 \\textrm{Var}[X] + b^2 \\textrm{Var}[Y] + 2ab \\textrm{Cov}[X,Y].\n$$\n\nIf $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = 0,$ and we recover the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX + bY] = a^2 \\textrm{Var}[X] + b^2 \\textrm{Var}[Y] .\n$$"] [17592186189421 :tutorial/content "Let's prove the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X+ Y] = \\textrm{Var}[X] + \\textrm{Var}[Y] + 2\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of variance and using the linearity of expectation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X+Y] & =\\text{E}[((X+Y)-\\text{E}[X+Y])^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[((X-\\text{E}[X])+(Y-\\text{E}[Y]))^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}+(Y-\\text{E}[Y])^{2}+2(X-\\text{E}[X])(Y-\\text{E}[Y])].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDistributing the expected value, and using the fact that $\\textrm{E}[aX] = a\\cdot \\textrm{E}[X]$ for constant $a,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X+Y] & =\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}+(Y-\\text{E}[Y])^{2}+2(X-\\text{E}[X])(Y-\\text{E}[Y])] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])^{2}]+\\text{E}[(Y-\\text{E}[Y])^{2}]+2\\cdot \\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])(Y-\\text{E}[Y])] \\\\  & =\\text{Var}[X]+\\text{Var}[Y]+2\\text{Cov}[X,Y]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required. \n\nThe proof for the difference between two random variables is similar."] [17592186188522 :activity/expected-seconds 780.0]] "retractions" [[17592186188065 :question/problem "For two random variables $X$ and $Y$, we have $\\textrm{Var}[X] = 3$, $\\textrm{Var}[Y] = 0.24$, and $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = 0.2$. Determine $\\textrm{Var}[X-Y]$."] [17592186188065 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X \\pm Y] = \\textrm{Var}[X] + \\textrm{Var}[Y] \\pm 2\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X - Y] = 3 + 0.24 - 2 \\cdot 0.2 = 2.84\n$$"] [17592186188067 :question/problem "For two random variables $X$ and $Y$, we have $\\textrm{Var}[X] = 5$, $\\textrm{Var}[Y] = 2$, and $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = 3$. Find $\\textrm{Var}[2X - 5Y]$."] [17592186188067 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX + bY] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X] + b^2\\textrm{Var}[Y] + 2ab\\,\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[2X - 5Y] &= 2^{2} \\cdot 5 + (-5)^{2} \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (-5) \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= 20 + 50 - 60 \\\\\n&= 10\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186188068 :question/problem "The joint probability mass function of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ |\n\nFind $\\textrm{Var}[5X+2Y]$."] [17592186188068 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX+bY] = a^2 \\textrm{Var}[X] + b^2 \\textrm{Var}[Y] + 2ab\\,\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]\n$$\n\nAlso, the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\textrm{E}[X^2] - \\textrm{E}[X]^2\n$$\n\nand the covariance of two random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = \\textrm{E}[XY] - \\textrm{E}[X] \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y]\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y$. These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ | $0.6$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.5$ | $0.5$ | $1$ |\n\nNow, we can find $\\textrm{Var}[X]$. If $X$ is defined on a sample space $S_X$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X] &= ∑_(x ∈ S_{X})x \\cdot f_{X}(x) \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 0.4 + 3 \\cdot 0.6 \\\\\n&= 1.8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[X^{2}] &= ∑_(x ∈ S_{X})x^{2} \\cdot f_{X}(x) \\\\\n&= 0^{2} \\cdot 0.4 + 3^{2} \\cdot 0.6 \\\\\n&= 5.4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X] &= E[X^{2}] - E[X]^{2} \\\\\n&= 5.4 - 1.8^{2} \\\\\n&= 2.16\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a sample space $S_Y$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y] &= ∑_(y ∈ S_{y})y \\cdot f_{Y}(y) \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 0.5 + 2 \\cdot 0.5 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[Y^{2}] &= ∑_(y ∈ S_{y})y^{2} \\cdot f_{Y}(y) \\\\\n&= 0^{2} \\cdot 0.5 + 2^{2} \\cdot 0.5 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[Y] &= E[Y^{2}] - E[Y]^{2} \\\\\n&= 2 - 1^{2} \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]$. We find $\\textrm{E}[XY]$ using the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nE[XY] &= ∑_((x, y) ∈ S)xy \\cdot f(x, y) \\\\\n&= 0 \\cdot 0 \\cdot f(0, 0) + 0 \\cdot 2 \\cdot f(0, 2) + 3 \\cdot 0 \\cdot f(3, 0) + 3 \\cdot 2 \\cdot f(3, 2) \\\\\n&= 0 + 0 + 0 + 6 \\cdot 0.2 \\\\\n&= 1.2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nCov[XY] &= E[XY] - E[X] \\cdot E[Y] \\\\\n&= 1.2 - 1.8 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&=-0.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[5X + 2Y] &= 5^{2} \\cdot 2.16 + 2^{2} \\cdot 1 + 2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 2 \\cdot (-0.6) \\\\\n&= 54 + 4 - 12 \\\\\n&= 46\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186188069 :question/problem "For three random variables $X_1,X_2$, and $X_3$, we have the following variances and covariances:\n\n$\\textrm{Var}[X_1] = 1, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_2] = 3, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[X_3] = 8 \\qquad$\n\n$\\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_2] = 2, \\qquad \\textrm{Cov}[X_1,X_3] = 4, \\qquad \\textrm{Cov}[X_2,X_3] = -5 \\qquad$\n\nFind $\\textrm{Var}[5X_1 + 2X_2 + 3X_3]$."] [17592186188069 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, \\ldots, X_n$ are random variables, and $a_1, \\ldots, a_n$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}\\left[\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} a_i X_i \\right] = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^{n} a_i^2 \\textrm{Var}[X_i] + 2\\sum\\limits_{i < j} a_i a_j \\textrm{Cov}[X_i,X_j]\n$$\n\nHence, for $n=3$ random variables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[a_{1}X_{1} + a_{2}X_{2} + a_{3}X_{3}] &= a_{1}^{2}Var[X_{1}] + a_{2}^{2}Var[X_{2}] + a_{3}^{2}Var[X_{3}] \\\\\n&= + 2a_{1}a_{2}Cov[X_{1}, X_{2}] + 2a_{1}a_{3}Cov[X_{1}, X_{3}] + 2a_{2}a_{3}Cov[X_{2}, X_{3}]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[5X_{1} + 2X_{2} + 3X_{3}] &= 5^{2} \\cdot 1 + 2^{2} \\cdot 3 + 3^{2} \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= + 2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 4 + 2 \\cdot 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot (-5) \\\\\n&= 25 + 12 + 72 + 40 + 120 - 60 \\\\\n&= 209\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186188780 :question/problem "For two random variables $X$ and $Y$, we have $Var[X] = 7$, $Var[Y] = 5$, and $Cov[X, Y] = 3$. Find $Var[X + Y]$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186188783 :question/problem "For two random variables $X$ and $Y$, we have $Var[X] = 5$, $Var[Y] = 11$, and $Cov[X, Y] = 4$. Find $Var[2X - 3Y]$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186188787 :question/problem "The joint probability mass function of the random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y = 1$ | $Y = 2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $X = 0$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X = 1$ | $0.3$ | $0.5$ |\n\nFind $Var[2X + 3Y]$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186188786 :question/problem "Consider two random variables $X$ and $Y$ with expectations $E[X] = 2$, $E[Y] = 4$, mixed moment $E[XY] = 10$, and second raw moments $E[X^{2}] = 9$ and $E[Y^{2}] = 20$. Find the variance of $2X - 5Y$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186188784 :question/problem "For three random variables $X_{1}, X_{2}$, and $X_{3}$, we have the following variances and covariances:\n\n$Var[X_{1}] = 5, Var[X_{2}] = 4, Var[X_{3}] = 2$\n\n$Cov[X_{1}, X_{2}] = 3, Cov[X_{1}, X_{3}] = 1, Cov[X_{2}, X_{3}] = 2$\n\nFind $Var[3X_{1} + 2X_{2} + 4X_{3}]$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186189413 :tutorial/content "Given two random variables $X$ and $Y$, the variance of the sum or difference of these variables is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X\\pm Y] = \\textrm{Var}[X] + \\textrm{Var}[Y] \\pm 2\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]\n$$\n\nwhere $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]$ is the covariance of $X$ and $Y$. We'll prove this formula at the end of the lesson.\n\nNote that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]= 0\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X\\pm Y] = \\textrm{Var}[X] + \\textrm{Var}[Y]\n$$"] [17592186189414 :tutorial/content "If $X$ and $Y$ are random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX + bY] = a^2 \\textrm{Var}[X] + b^2 \\textrm{Var}[Y] + 2ab \\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]\n$$\n\nIf $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = 0\n$$\n\nand we recover the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[aX + bY] = a^2 \\textrm{Var}[X] + b^2 \\textrm{Var}[Y]\n$$"] [17592186189421 :tutorial/content "Let's prove the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X+ Y] = \\textrm{Var}[X] + \\textrm{Var}[Y] + 2\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of variance and using the linearity of expectation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X + Y] &= E[((X + Y) - E[X + Y])^{2}] \\\\\n&= E[((X - E[X]) + (Y - E[Y]))^{2}] \\\\\n&= E[(X - E[X])^{2} + (Y - E[Y])^{2} + 2(X - E[X])(Y - E[Y])]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDistributing the expected value, and using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[aX] = a\\cdot \\textrm{E}[X]\n$$\n\nfor constant $a$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nVar[X + Y] &= E[(X - E[X])^{2} + (Y - E[Y])^{2} + 2(X - E[X])(Y - E[Y])] \\\\\n&= E[(X - E[X])^{2}] + E[(Y - E[Y])^{2}] + 2 \\cdot E[(X - E[X])(Y - E[Y])] \\\\\n&= Var[X] + Var[Y] + 2Cov[X, Y]\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nThe proof for the difference between two random variables is similar."]]}]